---
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>3.6 (Day 2) — Combat Engine RPG Build: Tactical Round &amp; Stances</h1>

<p>
    <strong>CED Topics 2.1, 2.3, 2.4, 2.5 · 90-Minute Design-First RPG Build (Day 2 of 2).</strong>
    Yesterday in <code>3.6a</code> you built <code>evaluateStrike</code>, <code>playerAttack</code>,
    and <code>monsterAttack</code> in <code>Combat.java</code>. Today you design and build the
    remaining three methods: damage mitigation (defend + shield clamping), live combat round
    sequencing, and tactical stance selection.
</p>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Design-First Workflow (Continue in Your Existing Files):</strong>
    <p style="margin-top:0.35rem; margin-bottom:0.4rem;">
        Keep working in your <code>Combat.java</code> alongside
        <a href="../data/Dice.java" download>Dice.java</a>. (Need a fresh stub? Download
        <a href="../data/Combat.java" download>Combat.java</a>.)
    </p>
    <ol style="margin-bottom:0.5rem;">
        <li><strong>Design before coding:</strong> Answer the Guided Design questions on shield
            healing bugs, pass-by-value returns, and tactical stance trade-offs.</li>
        <li><strong>Draft &amp; test in the sandbox:</strong> Draft each method below, click
            <strong>Copy</strong> to paste into <code>Combat.java</code>, and test Parts 3–5 with:</li>
    </ol>
    <pre><code>javac Dice.java Combat.java
java Combat</code></pre>
    <p style="margin-top:0.4rem; margin-bottom:0;">
        <strong>Step 3 (One-Shot Pass):</strong> Unlock <code>CombatCheck.java</code> below and aim
        to pass all <strong>32 checks across Parts 1–5</strong> on your first run.
    </p>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 3 · Damage Mitigation &amp; Two Clamps</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 3: <code>applyMitigatedDamage(int currentHP, int rawDamage, boolean isDefending, int flatShield)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            When an attack lands, armor and defending stances reduce incoming damage before HP drops.
            Without careful clamping, a strong shield could turn a weak hit into free healing—or a
            dragon strike could leave a hero with negative HP!
        </p>
        <h4>Mitigation Rules</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li>If <code>rawDamage &lt;= 0</code>, return <code>currentHP</code> unchanged.</li>
            <li>If <code>isDefending</code> is <code>true</code>, halve <code>rawDamage</code> with
                integer division (<code>rawDamage / 2</code>) first.</li>
            <li>Subtract <code>flatShield</code> to compute net damage, clamping net damage to
                <code>0</code> if negative.</li>
            <li>Subtract net damage from <code>currentHP</code>, clamping the final HP to
                <code>0</code> if negative.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>applyMitigatedDamage</code></h3>
        <p>Trace the two clamping edge cases before writing code:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>The Armor-Heal Trap &amp; Overkill Clamp:</strong> If a hero has
                <code>currentHP = 20</code> and <code>flatShield = 5</code>, and a Goblin hits for
                <code>rawDamage = 3</code> (not defending), what does
                <code>currentHP - (rawDamage - flatShield)</code> wrongly return if you forget to
                clamp net damage at <code>0</code>? And what happens on
                <code>currentHP = 12, rawDamage = 35, flatShield = 2</code> without the final HP clamp?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>Combat.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your complete <code>applyMitigatedDamage</code> method body, copy it into
                <code>Combat.java</code>, and check Part 3 in <code>java Combat</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 4 · Pass-By-Value &amp; Counterattack Gating</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 4: <code>combatRound(...)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            Orchestrates a single live exchange: the player strikes first via
            <code>playerAttack</code>, subtracting damage from <code>monsterHP</code>. Only if the
            monster survives (<code>monsterHP &gt; 0</code>) does it call <code>monsterAttack</code>.
        </p>
        <pre><code>public static int combatRound(int playerAtk, int monsterAC, int playerDmg,
                              int monsterAtk, int playerAC, int monsterDmg,
                              String monsterName, int monsterHP)</code></pre>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: Pass-By-Value &amp; Dead-Monster Guard</h3>
        <p>Reason through Java's primitive parameter passing:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Pass-By-Value Discovery:</strong> Why must <code>combatRound</code> both
                guard <code>if (monsterHP &gt; 0)</code> before the monster strikes AND
                <code>return monsterHP;</code>? If a caller writes
                <code>Combat.combatRound(..., goblinHP);</code> without assigning the return value,
                what happens to <code>goblinHP</code> in <code>main</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>Combat.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your complete <code>combatRound</code> method body, copy it into
                <code>Combat.java</code>, and check Part 4 in <code>java Combat</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 5 · Tactical Stance Synthesis</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~35 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 5: <code>resolveTacticalExchange(String stance, int playerHP, int monsterHP, int playerDmgDealt, int monsterRawDmg, int flatShield)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            Combines null-safe String comparison (<code>3.4</code>), boolean gating (<code>3.5</code>),
            and <code>applyMitigatedDamage</code> (Part 3) into one decision engine returning the
            <strong>player's remaining HP</strong>:
        </p>
        <table>
            <tr><th><code>stance</code></th><th>Player Strike on <code>monsterHP</code></th><th>If <code>monsterHP &gt; 0</code>, Player Takes:</th></tr>
            <tr><td><code>null</code> or <code>"flee"</code></td><td>No attack</td><td>No counterattack; return <code>playerHP</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>"attack"</code></td><td><code>-= playerDmgDealt</code></td><td><code>applyMitigatedDamage(playerHP, monsterRawDmg, false, flatShield)</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>"defend"</code></td><td><code>-= playerDmgDealt / 2</code></td><td><code>applyMitigatedDamage(playerHP, monsterRawDmg, true, flatShield)</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>"charge"</code></td><td><code>-= playerDmgDealt * 2</code></td><td>Shield dropped: <code>applyMitigatedDamage(playerHP, monsterRawDmg * 2, false, 0)</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Any other string</td><td><code>0</code> damage</td><td><code>applyMitigatedDamage(playerHP, monsterRawDmg, false, flatShield)</code></td></tr>
        </table>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: Null Safety &amp; Stance Trade-Off</h3>
        <p>Trace null safety and the tactical trade-off before coding:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Null Guard &amp; Stance Trace:</strong> Why must you check
                <code>stance == null</code> (or call <code>"flee".equals(stance)</code>) before
                <code>stance.equals(...)</code>? And for <code>playerHP = 25, monsterHP = 12,
                playerDmgDealt = 6, monsterRawDmg = 10, flatShield = 5</code>, trace the player's
                final HP under <code>"attack"</code>, <code>"defend"</code>, and
                <code>"charge"</code>—which stance leaves the most HP and why?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>Combat.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your complete <code>resolveTacticalExchange</code> method body, copy it into
                <code>Combat.java</code>, and verify all stances in <code>java Combat</code> before
                unlocking <code>CombatCheck.java</code> below?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Day 2 Milestone — Done When</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>All six methods run cleanly in <code>java Combat</code></li>
            <li>You unlock <code>CombatCheck.java</code> below and aim for <strong><code>32 passed, 0 failed</code></strong> across all 5 parts</li>
            <li>You have downloaded your <code>.json</code> answers file for Canvas</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-03/combat-system.html">Student RPG Guide: Combat System</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-03/comparing-objects.html">Comparing Objects</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-03/boolean-logic.html">Boolean Logic</a></li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
